Both commands show and configure network interfaces. ifconfig is the old tool (part of net-tools).
ip is the modern replacement (part of iproute2) and is installed by default on current distros.

ifconfig
----------
ifconfig                  # show all active interfaces
ifconfig -a               # show all interfaces, including down ones
ifconfig eth0             # show one interface
sudo ifconfig eth0 down   # disable interface
sudo ifconfig eth0 up     # enable interface

If you get “command not found”, install it:
--------------------------------------------
sudo apt install net-tools      # Ubuntu/Debian
sudo yum install net-tools      # RHEL/CentOS

ip
----
ip addr                   # show IP addresses (same as ip a)
ip addr show eth0         # one interface only
ip link                   # show interface status (up/down, MAC address)
ip route                  # show routing table and default gateway
sudo ip link set eth0 up  # enable interface
sudo ip link set eth0 down
sudo ip addr add 192.168.1.50/24 dev eth0   # temporarily add an IP

Comparison

Task	ifconfig	ip
Show IPs	ifconfig	ip addr
Interface up	ifconfig eth0 up	ip link set eth0 up
Routing table	route -n	ip route

Reading the output

eth0 or ens33: the network interface name (lo is the loopback)
inet: the IPv4 address
inet6: the IPv6 address
ether: the MAC address
UP / RUNNING: the interface is active

IP changes made with these commands are temporary and are lost after a reboot.
